commentBody:
@Chris

Me, too for the ENCLAVE / EXCLAVE error.  It reminded me of the short time I lived in Wichita, Kansas, a city that entirely surrounds Eastborough, the city-within-a-city enclave.  As I recall, it caused issues with the sharing of essential services such as firefighting, police, etc.

<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastborough,_Kansas#/media/File:Detailed_map_of_Eastborough,_Kansas.png" target="_blank">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastborough,_Kansas#/media/File:Detailed_map_of_Eastborough,_Kansas.png</a>

@Barry Ancona

If that's so, then it's the same for Cabinda and Kaliningrad.

My point was that Chris (San Diego) missed the obvious one.

@Ιασων

Let's see, it's made-up stuff, it denotes a specific meaning, one can find examples of EXCLAVEs on maps around the world.

That, my friend, is really a word.

@Don H

Thanks for taking the time to explain.  It's always interesting to see another solvers process.

I never heard of EXCLAVE either, but I was 100% sure of OXO, was 99.9999% sure there wasn't also a brand called ONO, and thought EXCLAVE looked reasonable.  The repetition of ONO would have pushed me away even if I considered it.  In the past, when a word was repeated, there was a very good reason.

@Francis
I also put EnCLAVE / OnO at first, but with ONO already locked in from a previous clue I was immediately suspicious and changed it. I see both EN- and EXclave as fine for a region contained within another country, like how a person who moves to live in a different country is both an immigrant and an emigrant. Exclave is pretty common among geography/border nerds, but you can use whichever fits best with the emphasis you want.
To Jason, I'd say remember that all words are made-up... It's not like ENCLAVE is a universal inevitability that formed during the Big Bang while EXCLAVE is a fake interloper plucked out of thin air...

@Robco

Patchouli is a plant in the mint family whose essential oil is used in perfumes to impart a woody, earthy, and musky scent. 
It's famous for being either loved or hated, with no middle ground.

@Andrzej

I wonder if she's related to the late Manny Nosowsky, a true poet and god of American crossword constuction, who died earlier this year, and holds the record for most crosswords published in the NYT (254).

<a href="https://www.xwordinfo.com/Author/Manny_Nosowsky" target="_blank">https://www.xwordinfo.com/Author/Manny_Nosowsky</a>

@Robco
Having come of age in the early 70s, I remain a sucker for patchouli.
And hippy women in general but yeah...patchouli...

@Robco
Ah, patchouli.
Having come of age in the early 70s I am still a sncker for patchoul. To my basal brain it means hippy women...

@Charles Peterson
I'd hate to think what a Dead show might've smelled like without it.

I found it odorific not aromatic but never-the-less it conjures memories.

@sotto voce

Thanks for the tune. Since you mentioned Supertramp, check out Roger Hodgson, his 12 string, and sax player at 4 a.m. on the Faroe Islands (apparently he wrote this song when he was 19). The joy in the crowd that his plane finally got there:

<a href="https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=CdTrqV6dt3o" target="_blank">https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=CdTrqV6dt3o</a>

@sotto voce

I'd never wondered about it before, but I just now decided I needed to know whence the name.

"The word supertramp means a supreme or superior wanderer/vagabond, originating from the 1908 book The Autobiography of a Super-Tramp by Welsh writer W. H. Davies."

@Weak
@Andrzej

Hey Andrzej, Weak: Sorry, I was being tongue in cheek. I know @Weak wasn’t disparaging Rian as a person, just his name from a crossword standpoint, and I’m sure he would be laughing about it if he read it. Sorry to both of you if it came off as scolding rather than amused, I actually think it was a pretty funny comment from Weak and I would say the same thing myself about ESAI Morales or ISSA Rae.

@Andrzej

You realize that “Family Feud” is just a play on a common expression, right? No feuding involved. And it’s not confrontational at all, just two families playing to win. The only thing I find objectionable about it (and most game shows in general) is the constant! loud! cheering!! to the point of mania. Makes me want to slip everyone a Valium and tell them to settle down, it’s just a game.

As for puns and dad jokes, I used to hate both. I thought they were beneath me. But now I kind of love them. I’ve come to appreciate the wordplay involved and recognize that a finely crafted pun is harder than it looks. (Hats off to Munster Mike!) I’ve also learned the value of allowing yourself to just be silly sometimes. It’s freeing.

@BM

The column is entitled "Concerning Hearing", and hearings take place in court - that's how I understand it.

Also, dad jokes should be considered a felony, so it still tracks...

@Andrzej

One certain thing about coming out strongly against puns is that you are setting yourself up to be inundated by them :-)

(Side note--I almost typed "unindated"--I never noticed that word starts with two prepositions.

@lucky13
Some courtroom artists are so meh, but Christine Cornell is really great. I wasn't familiar with her before this. Thanks for the link.

The No. 1 courtroom artist in NY when I was growing up was Ida Libby Dengrove, and I was so taken by her work. But I didn't know that she also did a lot of Jewish art, so I was shocked when I noticed that a print of hers was hanging over the buffet in my grandparents' dining room!

@Dan 
The other day, upon the stair,
I saw a man who wasn't there. 
He wasn't there again today; 
I really wish he'd go away.

@Matt 

I don’t think she was disparaging males, just justifying her use of the pronoun.
And as far as the difference in posts, @TimesRita did not reference age at all, just her distaste for the theme; it was pretty harsh but NOT personal. “OK Boomer” was personal and ageist. I’m not saying it was flag-worthy, but I think she is justified in being upset that so many people upvoted it and so few called it out. And it certainly is appalling to me that her post yesterday was taken down and I’d like to hear how any moderator thought that her questioning that was inappropriate.

@Matt

I hear you, and as you know was more on the side of defending the younger generation in those discussions. But I reread @TimesRita’s post and she was very careful not to bring age or class into it at all, just saying that she personally hated video games and thought it was too niche. It didn’t warrant the response and though insults were flying in al directions they weren’t from her.

Be that as it may, my bigger concern right now is that her post yesterday was taken down. Agree or disagree, she made very appropriate points about it and it is worthy of discussion in this forum and should NOT have been removed.

@Steve L
Now that gets my dander up! Like many with legal training, I am primarily irritated by procedural unfairness. 
Of course, this results in the situation of the pathetic innocent on their way to the scaffold, shouting "I didn't do it." To which the executioner replies: "You had a fair trial. Innocence isn't relevant any more. Any last words?"

@Barry Ancona

Yes, I know it A TUE--sday puzzle. And thanks for the AURAL hint.

STATUETTE still fails for me.

@Barry Ancona

For me, the central syllable is "softer" than "choo". More like "tyoo". And with the stress on the last syllable, the word simply doesn't suggest a sneeze (to me). 

In any case, that entry was easy enough. Having no clue about CHOCO tacos was my demise today.

@Barry Ancona 

And how many instances of crossing two Spanish clues on a Tuesday, Barry? 

You wouldn’t feel the need to defend these puzzles so much if you were actually confident in their ability to stand on their own against scrutiny. The lady doth protest too much, etc.

@Barry Ancona

I know - which one would have been better HERE (sorry about the caps, I don't know of a ways to caps/italicize/boldface)?

Taking it being Tuesday into consideration.

@KB
Further to Barry's comment, the OXO kitchen brand company was founded in 1990 and most of the clues since then were clues to it and fewer to tic tac toe. Frances would have liked how it was clued in the pre-Shortzian era, which was some form of Chemical pre-fix.
I note that it's never been clued to the British food brand nor the lovely OXO Tower in London. Hi, Jane.

@Vaer

Please consider which clue context results in  a more Tuesday-appropriate clues and answers crossing.

Those will be my last words on the subject.

@NanuNanu
Never heard the Mick Jagger addition!  Wish my dad was alive so I could tell him!

My dad had a different Mick Jagger joke in involving "Get off my Cloud" that was a dad joke in a different sense (i.e., not appropriate for kids due to sexual content and spurious stereotyping against Scots).

