Huh?  Huh?

@Francis

I saw a documentary about that town recently, I wonder if it's the same with a different name? It was fantastic. It's on YouTube for free and is called "Exposing the Myth of Southern Charm".

<a href="https://youtu.be/jHAauml9rV4?is=QbWCzIHtyB8kqhij" target="_blank">https://youtu.be/jHAauml9rV4?is=QbWCzIHtyB8kqhij</a>

@Asher B. are all Dad jokes puns? or vice versa? I don't know. 

I agree with responses that context and intent are important in humor, and not to take offense where none was intended. I do think it's possible to become too PC police-y, but some responses talked about jokes that invoke stereotypes of other groups - blondes, Jews, Poles, etc. that often cross the line. 

So back to puns - I am delighted by them, and - bonus - they don't require a victim. Also spoonerisms - The Rev. Spooner is long since passed and can't take offense. Malapropisms - the fictitious Mrs. Malaprop won't complain. Mondegreens? I doubt not that "Lady Mondegreen" would be happy to have them named after her, if she existed. So far from being the "lowest form of humor" I think they are the best.

